--- !u!114 &6892577655863974408
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 6892577655863974411}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 3ff6acaa8b9f1994499dd387e6188532, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  Comments: "This demonstrates VR 360 rendering with post processing applied in a
    second pass.\n\nWhy Two Camera Passes?\nSince post processing effects often introduce
    visible seams in cubemaps, this setup overcomes that by re-rendering the final
    output with a separate camera to apply post processing. \n\n360 Render Without
    Post Processing?\nThere is no setup for plain 360 rendering without post processing
    due to an internal gamma issue which slows down rendering considerably to correct.
    This is resolved by using the 2-pass camera method, so if you wish to render
    without post processing, simply use this setup but deactivate the post processing
    volume.\n\nTo preserve HDR quality floating space render textures are recommended.\n\nTo
    use this setup in your own scene, simply add this prefab in place of your camera.
    The examples are configured to render 2K which you may customize. Make sure changes
    to the render resolution match the render textures in use, or errors may occur."
  Warning: 'Certain rendering features such as screen space effects, some shaders,
    and post processing effects may not be supported in cubemap rendering setups.
    This can result in noticeable seams where the cubemap is stitched. To avoid these
    issues, test early on and decide on rendering features that do work with cubemap
    rendering.


    Layer Masks

    The built-in ''Ignore Raycast'' layer is used
    to separate the rendering of the MainCamera from the post processing camera,
    volume, and image plane. Note also that the MainCamera exlcudes the ''Ignore
    Raycast'' layer in its culling mask so that it doesn''t render the image plane.


    You
    may change the layer to any that works best for your project, though make sure
    that both the Culling Mask and Volume Mask on the cameras are set to match the
    layer assigned, otherwise you may not get the results desired.'
  URLTitle: Render To Disk Documentation
  URL: https://axongenesis.gitbook.io/timeflow/reference/behaviors/rendering/render-to-disk
  URL2Title: 
  URL2: 
